avaScript Practice Test
// ===============================

// QUESTION 1: Variables
// Declare a variable called 'city' and assign your favorite city name to it.
// Then log: "My favorite city is <city>".



// QUESTION 2: Functions
// Write a function called 'addNumbers' that takes two numbers and returns their sum.


// Example: addNumbers(5, 7) ➞ 12



// QUESTION 3: Loops
// Use a for loop to print all numbers from 1 to 10.


// QUESTION 4: Arrays
// Create an array named 'fruits' with 3 fruits.
// Then use a loop to print each fruit on a new line.


// QUESTION 5: Objects
// Create an object called 'student' with properties: name, age, and course.
// Log a sentence like: "<name> is <age> years old and studies <course>."


// QUESTION 6: Conditional Statements
// Write a function that takes a number and prints:
// "Even" if it’s even, and "Odd" if it’s odd.


// QUESTION 7: ES6 Feature
// Convert the following function into an arrow function:
// function multiply(a, b) {
//     return a * b;
// }



// QUESTION 8: DOM Manipulation (Browser Only)
// In HTML, create a <button id="clickMe">Click me</button>
// Write JavaScript that changes the button text to "Clicked!" when it's clicked.


// HINT: Use document.getElementById("clickMe").addEventListener(...)


// QUESTION 9: String Methods
// Write code that takes the string "JavaScript is fun!" and logs it in all uppercase.


// QUESTION 10: Bonus Challenge 🎁
// Write a function that reverses a string (without using built-in reverse()).
// Example: reverseString("hello") ➞ "olleh"



